<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eustace Grenier</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1123</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Eustace Garnier</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eustace Grenier was a knight from northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> who became one of the great barons of the crusader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a>. King Baldwin I granted him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a> after its surrender in 1110, and he also held Caesarea from about the same time; his descendants kept both lordships for generations. In 1123, while King Baldwin II was a prisoner of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artuqid-dynasty">Artuqids</a>, Eustace served as regent of the kingdom, dying later that year.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Eustace Grenier, called Eustachius Granarius or Garnerius in the charters, was born around 1070. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> poem by a contemporary, the Versus de viris illustribus diocesis Tarvanensis, names him among the knights of the diocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/therouanne">Thérouanne</a> who went with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-i-of-jerusalem">Baldwin of Boulogne</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a>. The historian Alan V. Murray concludes from it that he was a military official at the castle of Beaurainville in the County of Saint-Pol, a rear-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of Eustace III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boulogne-sur-mer">Boulogne</a>, and that he most probably came on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a> in the retinue of Hugh II of Saint-Pol. His surname suggests that he or an ancestor managed a storehouse.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His first recorded act in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a> is his part in the Third Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramla">Ramla</a> in August 1105. Baldwin I sent him with Pagan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haifa">Haifa</a> to arrange a meeting with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bertrand-of-toulouse">Bertrand of Toulouse</a>, William Jordan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerdanya">Cerdanya</a> and Tancred, regent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>; the leaders reached a compromise near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripoli</a> in June 1109, and their united armies forced the city to surrender on 26 June.</p><p>Eustace took part in Baldwin's campaigns against Shaizar in 1111 and paid for siege machines at the unsuccessful siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> the same year. His two lordships and his wife's dowry made him the most powerful nobleman of the kingdom, and he was one of four secular lords at the Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nablus">Nablus</a> held by Baldwin II and Patriarch Warmund on 16 January 1120.</p><p>After the Artuqid prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belek-ghazi">Belek Ghazi</a> captured Baldwin II on 18 April 1123, an assembly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> called by Warmund elected Eustace constable and bailiff of the kingdom. He sent troops to help the king at Kharpurt, but the fortress had been retaken before they arrived. In May 1123 a Fatimid army invaded from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkelon">Ascalon</a>, and Eustace defeated it near Ibelin on 29 May.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Eustace died on 15 June 1123 and was buried in the Church of Saint Mary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latins</a>. William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> called him a wise and prudent man, with great experience in military matters. He had married Emma, niece of Patriarch Arnulf of Chocques, who gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jericho">Jericho</a> as her dowry. Their twin sons Gerard and Walter succeeded him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a> and Caesarea, and the family held Sidon until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julian-grenier">Julian Grenier</a> sold it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Knights Templar</a> after 1260.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eustace Grenier” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The city became the seat of a lordship, held from about 1110 by <b>Eustace Grenier</b> and his descendants.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-caesarea">Lordship of Caesarea</a></li><li><blockquote>The city was granted to <b>Eustace Grenier</b>, and his descendants held it as one of the kingdom's chief lordships.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-sidon">Lordship of Sidon</a></li></ul></section>
